These are such typical deliberately misleading Dario Amodei arguments! Here is a short refutal (with the help of GPT-5.6 pro) of this intellectually dishonest letter. I’ll post much more detailed long version later and will be talking with @peterottsjo tomorrow to breakdown his biosecurity arguments.

Anthropic’s essay is not a defense of open weights but a carefully worded attempt to preserve the appearance of openness while constructing a regulatory moat around frontier AI: it praises open models only when they are weak enough not to threaten proprietary incumbents, then invokes speculative “nightmare scenarios” to justify chip embargoes, anti-distillation rules, mandatory testing, centralized monitoring, and government-enforced barriers that disproportionately burden open developers, startups, researchers, and foreign competitors while protecting Anthropic’s own API business. 

Its core premises are either unsupported or internally contradictory: 

> a secret military model would ignore global testing rules; 

>China’s AI progress cannot be reduced to access to American chips because algorithms, domestic hardware, engineering efficiency, and specialization also matter; 

>distillation is a standard industry technique, not inherently theft or a national-security offense; 

> and current safety evaluations are too immature, gameable, and context-dependent to function as reliable release licenses. 

The essay also counts every possible misuse enabled by open access while largely ignoring its benefits, independent auditing, privacy, scientific reproducibility, local control, defensive cybersecurity, resilience, competition, and freedom from corporate censorship or withdrawal. 

Most tellingly, Anthropic asks the public to accept that policies which restrict rivals, preserve closed-model control, raise compliance costs, and protect its commercial lead are motivated only by safety; whether or not that is Amodei’s sincere belief, the practical effect is unmistakable: concentrate frontier intelligence in a handful of powerful corporations and let those same corporations help decide who else is allowed to build, study, or release it.

